  "note": "The getomni schema asks for one string (the largest employment category) and a four-row male/female series rounded to the nearest thousand — thirteen leaf values, and the rounding erases most of the difficulty. The page actually carries two full stacked-bar figures: Figure 20 with six employment-level series across four years, and Figure 21 with six age bands x two genders x four years plus a per-row total. Extended to cover both figures in full, plus the figure titles, chart type, y-axis maxima and printed axis labels, the legend labels verbatim (en dashes, not hyphens), the F=Female / M=Male key, and the page number and report footer. Provenance is unusual for this document and worth stating plainly: no numeric value is printed anywhere on the page — every bar value must come from either the chart geometry or the dataset's own human-authored table annotation. The series values here are taken from that annotation (`chart_003_gt.txt` / `chart_003_gt.json`, which the dataset labels 'human-authored markdown GT'), not read off pixels by me, so they are recorded under dataset_human. Everything that is literally printed on the page — titles, axis labels, legend text, footer — was verified against the 14 saved engine outputs and sits under model_verified; the legend and axis strings corroborate at 6-9 of 14 because half the engines drop chart furniture entirely, which is exactly the behaviour this document should be penalising. The dataset's 'topWorkforceSector' and 'workforceByGender' are preserved unchanged, including its rounded thousands, even though the unrounded totals (e.g. 8,200 male in 2020) are also present in 'ageAndGenderBreakdown'.",
  "extended_annotator": "claude-opus-5",
  "field_provenance": {
    "dataset_human": [
      "topWorkforceSector", "workforceByGender",
      "employmentLevelBreakdown", "ageAndGenderBreakdown"
    ],
